.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ppk
creates Peios packages, stores them in an artifact directory, copies them
from one artifact directory to another, composes them into closures, and
lays closures out as the root of an environment or runs commands with them
at
.IR /usr .
.PP
A package is a manifest that lists every path in it, with each file's
content stored separately as a blob named by its SHA-256 hash.
Paths are relative to
.IR /usr :
a package's
.I bin/hello
is
.IR /usr/bin/hello .
The package's identity is the SHA-256 hash of its manifest, written as
.B sha256:
followed by the hash in lowercase hexadecimal.
.PP
A
.I package
argument is an identifier of the form
.IB name @ version\c
.RB [ ?arch= \c
.IR arch ].
.I name
is reverse-DNS: the domain of whoever owns the software, backwards, then
the product, as in
.BR dev.peios.hello ,
.BR org.gnu.gcc\-c++ ,
and
.BR io.github.eudev\-project.eudev .
It is at most 64 characters long and has at least three labels, separated by
dots.
Each label consists of lowercase ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.B +
and
.BR \- ,
starts with a letter or digit, ends with a letter, digit, or
.BR + ,
and does not contain
.BR \-\- .
The first label consists only of letters, and neither of the first two
contains a
.BR + .
.PP
.I version
is
.RI [ epoch\c
.BR : ]\c
.IB upstream \- revision\c
, as in
.BR 1.26.2\-3 ,
.BR 1.0\-rc.1\-1 ,
and
.BR 2:0.5.0\-1 .
.I epoch
is optional and
.I revision
is required; both are decimal numbers without leading zeros, and the
revision is at least 1.
.I upstream
is what follows the epoch, up to the last hyphen.
It consists of ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.BR . ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
and
.BR ~ ,
and starts with a letter or digit.
Versions are ordered by epoch, then upstream version, then revision.
The upstream versions are compared a run of digits or a run of letters at a
time: digits as numbers, and letters by byte, except that
.BR dev ,
.B alpha
or
.BR a ,
.B beta
or
.BR b ,
.BR pre ,
and
.BR rc ,
in any case, mark a pre-release and sort in that order before the release.
Anything after a
.B ~
is a pre-release too, so
.B 1.0~rc1\-1
comes before
.BR 1.0\-1 .
This is the grammar and order of PSPU 5.5 and 5.6.
.PP
.I arch
consists of ASCII letters, digits, and the characters
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- ,
and
.BR ~ ,
and starts with a letter or digit.
Without
.BR ?arch= ,
the architecture is the one
.B ppk
was built for.
Use
.B noarch
for content that does not depend on the architecture.
No other qualifier is accepted, and
.B arch
can be given only once.
.SH PROFILES
An environment profile is a TOML file that says what an environment asks
for, for
.B compose\-env
and
.BR run :
.PP
.RS
.EX
include = ["layout.toml"]
dirs = ["proc", "sys", "tmp"]

[packages]
"net.busybox.busybox" = "1.37.0\-1"
"dev.peios.prelude" = ">= 0.0.7"

[symlinks]
"init" = ":sbin/prelude"
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B packages
maps each package asked for to the versions that will do, as a constraint;
a bare version asks for exactly that version, and the empty string for any.
.B #
and an index or channel, as in
.B """#stable"""
or
.BR """#stable >= 1.36""" ,
asks for the version that index holds, as under REPOSITORIES.
A profile holds requests, not their answer: composing it resolves them, with
what they need, and the closure's inventory records the packages chosen.
Every package is for the architecture
.B arch
gives, or the one
.B ppk
was built for.
.B symlinks
maps each link's path to its target, written as what follows
.IB path :
in
.BR \-\-symlink ,
so that a leading colon stands for
.IR usr .
.B dirs
lists empty directories.
.B include_artifacts = true
gives a composed root its packages as
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
does.
Every key is optional, and any other key is an error.
.PP
.B include
lists other profiles, called fragments, whose contents the profile holds as
well, such as a root layout that several profiles share.
Each path is relative to the directory of the file that names it, and a
fragment can include others.
A fragment's
.B arch
is for its own packages.
What two of them hold alike is held once.
It is an error for them to disagree: to give two versions of one package,
packages for two architectures, two targets for one link, or a link and a
directory at one path, and the error names both files.
.B include_artifacts
is true if any of them says so.
A profile that includes itself, directly or through others, is an error.
Several profiles given to
.B compose\-env
or
.B run
are merged in the same way.
.PP
The packages named with
.B \-p
are added to the profile's, and the links and directories named with
.B \-\-symlink
and
.B \-\-dir
to its own.
Another version of a package the profile holds, or a link at a path the
profile links elsewhere, is an error.
.SH REPOSITORIES
A repository, an artifact directory that
.B repo update
has published, can be asked for packages by index or channel:
.B #stable
after a name takes the version the channel
.B stable
leads to, and the dependencies and requirements of a package chosen that way
are settled from the same index, so that a request for a release takes the
release's versions of what it needs too.
A package asked for by name or with an exact ID is settled as usual.
The request
.B se.curl.curl#stable
fails if that index holds no version of
.B se.curl.curl
that satisfies it, or if no repository among the artifact directories
publishes
.BR stable .
.PP
A version a repository has yanked is chosen only by a request for exactly
it.
A request a deleted version would have satisfied fails, and says why it was
deleted.
.PP
An inventory records the index of each request that named one, as in
.BR "[* #stable >=8]" .
.SH METADATA
Besides its files, a package's manifest can say what the package needs and
offers, and describe it.
.B ppk pack
records what its options give, and the manifest's identity covers it.
.PP
A dependency is on another package, by name.
A requirement is on a capability: a name that something provides, such as
the soname
.BR libc.so.6 ,
the command
.BR sh ,
or
.BR pkgconfig(zlib) .
A capability's name is 2 to 128 characters: ASCII letters of either case,
digits, and the characters
.BR \- ,
.BR . ,
.BR + ,
and
.BR _ ,
starting with a letter or digit and ending with one or with
.BR + ;
or a
.IB namespace ( argument )\c
, whose namespace is lowercase letters and digits starting with a letter, and
whose argument may also hold
.B :
and
.BR / .
.PP
Each dependency and requirement has a
.IR constraint :
one or more expressions separated by commas, all of which must hold.
An expression is one of
.BR = ,
.BR > ,
.BR >= ,
.BR < ,
.BR <= ,
and
.BR != ,
followed by a version, and a version alone means
.BR = .
The version may leave out its revision, and then the revision is not
compared:
.B >= 2.44
holds for
.BR 2.44\-3 .
An empty constraint holds for any version.
A provided capability has a version, which may also leave out its revision,
or none.
.PP
A claim is a link, at an absolute path, that the package provides besides
its files, which are all under
.IR /usr .
Composing creates it: one package in an environment provides each claimed
path, so two that claim one path can't be composed together.
.PP
.B ppk resolve
chooses packages that satisfy them, and writes their profile.
.SH SECURITY METADATA
A file or directory of a package can carry security metadata, which its
manifest entry records and the package's identity covers.
.PP
A security descriptor replaces the one the file or directory would
otherwise get.
It is a self-relative security descriptor with an owner, a group, and a
DACL, given to
.B \-\-descriptor
in hexadecimal and written in the manifest in lowercase.
Its bytes are kept as given:
.B ppk
checks only that its parts lie within it, and the kernel judges what it
grants.
A symbolic link can't have one.
A directory with one is listed in the manifest even if it has contents.
.PP
A signature is a file's PIP signature, which the kernel reads from the
file's
.B security.peios.sig
attribute before it loads the file as firmware.
.B ppk pack
takes it from the file beside the one it signs, named with
.B .peios.sig
added, which is not packed as a file of its own.
It must be 3310 bytes, starting with the version byte 1, and the file it
signs must be a regular file that is not ELF, since an ELF file carries
its signature in its
.B .peios.sig
section.
No other path may end in
.BR .peios.sig .
The signature is stored as a blob, and the manifest names it by its hash.
.PP
A file with security metadata is stored as a file of its own that carries
it, so every hard link to it carries it too: two files with the same
content and different metadata are two stored files.
On Peios,
.B ppk
gives a stored file, a copy, or a directory its descriptor with the
.B kacs_set_sd
system call and its signature as an attribute when it creates it, before anything else
can reach it, and fails if it can't.
Elsewhere it writes them as the
.B security.peios.sd
and
.B security.peios.sig
attributes where the system lets it, which usually needs privilege.
Where it doesn't,
.B ppk
leaves them out and warns, giving the number of files and directories that
lack them; their manifests still record them.
.PP
A stored file that already exists must carry the signature its entry
names: one that lacks it is given it, and one with another is an error.
On Peios, a stored file's descriptor can't be read back as it was given, so
a file stored elsewhere without its descriptor is not given it when it is
linked; fetch the package on Peios, or copy its files, instead.
.SH DERIVATION
.B ppk pack
also reads each ELF object among the files, and adds to the manifest what it
needs and provides.
An object's
.B DT_NEEDED
sonames become requirements, for any version, and its
.B DT_SONAME
becomes a capability the package provides.
A soname the package provides is not also required.
Files under
.IR src/ ,
which hold source rather than installed objects, are not read, nor are
symbolic links, which alias an object packed at its own path.
Other files that are not ELF are passed over.
.PP
For the glibc libraries
.RB ( ld\-linux\-x86\-64.so.2 ,
.BR libanl.so.1 ,
.BR libc.so.6 ,
.BR libdl.so.2 ,
.BR libm.so.6 ,
.BR libmvec.so.1 ,
.BR libnsl.so.1 ,
.BR libpthread.so.0 ,
.BR libresolv.so.2 ,
.BR librt.so.1
and
.BR libutil.so.1 )
and for
.BR libgcc_s.so.1 ,
whose symbol versions are numbered as their package is, the highest symbol
version that any object needs is a floor.
An object needing
.B GLIBC_2.34
of
.B libc.so.6
gives the requirement
.BR "libc.so.6 >= 2.34" .
A package that provides one of these libraries provides it at its own
version, such as
.BR 2.44\-1 .
.PP
The versions of
.B libstdc++.so.6
are not numbered as gcc is, so each is an exact capability instead:
.BI elfver(libstdc++.so.6: version )\c
\&.
A package providing
.B libstdc++.so.6
provides each version it defines, and an object needing a version requires
it, unless the need is weak.
.PP
What derivation finds joins what the options give.
Where both name the same capability, the option's entry is kept, unless it
gives no version or holds for any version, when the derived one is.
There are two exceptions.
A requirement on one of the floor libraries for anything but any version is
refused, because its floor comes only from the payload.
A provide of one at a version other than the package's is refused.
Either refusal names the written entry and what the payload gives, and
nothing is written.
.PP
An object that cannot be read, or whose version tables are inconsistent, is
an error naming it.
So is a package providing
.B libstdc++.so.6
that needs a version of it that it does not define.
A shared library named
.BI lib name .so
with no soname gives a warning on standard error, and nothing can depend on
it.
.PP
The same files give the same requirements and provides, in the same
manifest.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Print the current closure's full package IDs, one per line in byte order,
from
.IR /usr/inventory .
No artifact directory is needed.
The inventory records the closure's identity, then each package's full ID
and identity and what wants it, as described under FILES.
A malformed inventory or a mismatched closure identity is an error.
The package IDs describe the recorded packages; checking the closure hash
does not authenticate their manifests or the files in the tree.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-all ,
list every package indexed by the artifact directory's refs, sorted by full ID.
Each line is a marker, a space, and the full ID.
The marker is
.B *
when both the ID and identity match a package in the current closure, and a
space otherwise, so every ID starts in the same column.
Without an inventory, no package is marked;
without
.BR \-\-all ,
a missing inventory is an error explaining that this
.I /usr
was not composed by a ppk that records one.
.TP
.BI pack " package path"
Pack the directory
.I path
as
.I package
and print the package's identity on standard output.
.IP
Regular files, symbolic links, and empty directories are recorded.
Directories with contents are implied by them, unless given a descriptor.
A symbolic link is recorded with its target exactly as it reads and is never followed.
Of a file's permissions, only whether any execute bit is set is recorded.
.IP
.B ppk pack
refuses a directory that holds anything else, such as a socket or a device;
a file with its set-user-ID or set-group-ID bit set;
or a path or link target that is not valid UTF-8.
It also refuses an artifact directory inside
.IR path .
The top-level path
.I inventory
is reserved for ppk, whether a file, directory, or symbolic link.
Packages cannot claim
.I /usr/inventory
or paths beneath it.
.IP
The manifest also records the metadata the options give, as
.B METADATA
describes, and each file's descriptor and signature, as
.B SECURITY METADATA
describes.
A broken rule in it is a usage error, and nothing is written.
It adds what the files' ELF objects need and provide, as
.B DERIVATION
describes.
.IP
If
.I package
is already packed with the same contents and metadata,
.B ppk pack
succeeds without changing anything.
If it is packed with different contents or metadata,
.B ppk pack
fails and writes nothing: a packed version is never replaced.
.TP
.BI fetch " request"
Resolve
.I request
and its dependencies and requirements against the artifact directory named by
.B \-\-from
and copy every chosen package into the artifact directory.
Print one identity per fetched package on standard output, in package-name order.
A request is a bare name, a name with a constraint as for
.BR resolve ,
or an exact package ID, including its version's spelling and architecture.
A name followed by
.B #
and an index or channel of a repository, as in
.BR se.curl.curl#stable ,
asks for the version that index holds, optionally with a constraint after
it; what that version needs comes from the same index too, as described
under REPOSITORIES.
Unqualified requests use
.BR \-\-arch ,
or the architecture ppk was built for.
An explicitly qualified ID can select its own architecture.
All packages in one resolution must be for one architecture.
Resolution errors name the missing or conflicting requirement and occur
before copying starts.
With
.BR \-\-no\-deps ,
copy exactly the package ID given, without resolving anything it needs;
bare names and constraints are not accepted in this mode.
Blobs already present are not copied.
.IP
Without
.BR \-\-from ,
fetch from the repositories configured in
.BR \-\-config\-dir ,
.I /conf/ppk
unless given, as under REPOSITORIES.
A request for a package by a bare name or a constraint, naming no index and
no exact version, follows the index or channel its repository tracks.
Every package name belongs to one repository: one that two repositories
publish is an error, for a request or anything it needs.
A repository whose configuration gives a public key must have signed its
pointer file with it, and none may publish a lower sequence than this machine
has seen of it, which is kept in
.BR \-\-state\-dir ,
.I /var/state/ppk
unless given.
.IP
Nothing read from the source is trusted.
The source's ref must hold exactly one identity and a newline.
The manifest must hash to that identity, be in exactly the form
.B ppk pack
writes, name the chosen package,
and contain only relative paths with no empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
component.
Every blob copied must hash to the name the manifest gives it.
A stored file takes only its content from the source: it is given the
security metadata its entry records.
If a check fails,
.B ppk fetch
stops without writing the manifest or the ref.
Blobs it copied before the failure are named by their content and are kept.
.IP
If
the chosen package
is already present with the same identity,
.B ppk fetch
skips it without writes or output, while still resolving its dependencies.
With
.BR \-\-no\-deps ,
it copies any missing files and prints the identity as before.
If it is present with a different identity,
.B ppk fetch
fails and writes nothing, unless
.B \-\-replace \-\-no\-deps
is given. Earlier packages fetched successfully remain if a later fetch fails.
.IP
These checks prove that the package matches the source's ref.
They do not prove who published it, so fetch only from sources you trust,
or from a configured repository with a key: then the package must also be in
the repository's catalog, at the identity the catalog gives, and the
catalog is reached from the pointer file the key signed, so a package the
repository didn't publish, or a ref changed since, is refused.
.TP
.BI closure " package\fR...\fP"
Compose the packages, each already in the artifact directory, into a
closure: one directory tree, the contents of a
.IR /usr .
Each package is asked for exactly.
Print the closure's identity on standard output.
.IP
Each built tree includes
.IR inventory ,
also written as
.I usr/inventory
by
.BR compose\-env .
When mounted by
.BR run ,
this becomes the command's own
.IR /usr/inventory .
An existing tree without an inventory is reused unchanged as a closure
built by an older ppk; compose a fresh root or use a separate closure
directory to obtain an inventory.
.IP
Every package file is a hard link to its blob, in the form its entry
needs, which carries its security metadata, or a copy of it with
.BR \-\-copy\-files ;
symbolic links and directories are created in the tree, each directory
with its descriptor, and so is each link a package claims under
.IR /usr .
A closure is only
.IR /usr ,
so links claimed elsewhere are left out of it.
The closure's manifest is its inventory without the first line: its
packages, sorted and without duplicates, and what wants each.
The closure's identity is the SHA-256 hash of that manifest, so the order
and repetition of the arguments do not matter, but the same packages asked
for differently are another closure.
Every package must be wanted by a request or by another package in it.
An existing closure is reused, however its files were placed.
A tree is built in a temporary directory and renamed into place when
complete.
.IP
.B ppk closure
fails, writing nothing, if two packages give one path different entries,
even entries that differ only in their security metadata,
if a path lies beneath a file or symbolic link of another package, if two
packages claim one path, even with one target, if a claimed path is also in
a package's files or lies beneath one, or if a package claims
.I /usr
itself.
It fails, leaving nothing behind, if a hard link cannot be made because the
closure directory is not on the same mount as the artifact directory, or if
a blob it copies does not match its hash.
.TP
.B repo update
Publish the repository, an artifact directory: write its catalog, every
version its refs hold, and the index
.BR leading ,
the newest current version of each package for each architecture, then sign
its pointer file, one higher in sequence.
A package whose dependencies or requirements
.B leading
can't satisfy is left out of it, with a warning, and so is anything left
needing it; with
.BR \-\-strict ,
that is an error and nothing is published.
A version yanked or deleted stays so.
.IP
Every
.B repo
command that publishes signs with the ML-DSA-65 private key in the PEM file
.BR \-\-key ,
or in
.B $PPK_REPO_KEY
if that isn't given, the kind of key
.B openssl genpkey \-algorithm ML\-DSA\-65
writes, and prints the pointer file it published.
.TP
.BI "repo snapshot" " name"
Make
.I name
a new index holding what the index or channel
.B \-\-from
holds,
.B leading
unless given.
A snapshot never changes, so
.I name
must be new.
.TP
.BI "repo channel" " name target"
Make the channel
.I name
follow the index or channel
.IR target .
With
.BR \-\-ended ,
mark it as one that won't move again.
Indexes and channels share one namespace, and every channel must reach an
index.
.TP
.BI "repo yank" " package"
Take the version
.I package
back: new resolutions don't choose it, but a request for exactly it still
does.
With
.BR \-\-undo ,
restore it.
Publishes again, so
.B leading
follows.
.TP
.BI "repo delete" " package"
Delete the version
.IR package :
its ref, its manifest, and every file of it that no other manifest names.
The catalog keeps a tombstone with the reason
.BR \-\-reason
gives, one line, so that a resolution that needs it can say why it's gone.
Publishes again.
.TP
.B repo show
Print the repository's pointer file.
With
.BR \-\-public\-key ,
its signature must be that key's.
.TP
.BI "repo add" " name path"
Add the repository at
.I path
to those this machine fetches from, as
.IR name ,
writing its configuration in
.BR \-\-config\-dir ,
.I /lcl/conf/ppk
unless given, which
.I /conf/ppk
merges with what the system ships.
With
.BR \-\-public\-key ,
its pointer file must be signed with that key; with
.BR \-\-track ,
a request for a bare name follows that index or channel, such as
.BR stable .
A repository of the same name is replaced.
.TP
.BI "repo remove" " name"
Stop fetching from the repository
.IR name .
.TP
.B repo list
Print each repository this machine fetches from: its name, its path, the
index or channel it follows, and the sequence and signing time of its
pointer file, checked as
.B fetch
checks it.
.TP
.B repo public\-key
Print the public key of
.BR \-\-key ,
as
.B ml\-dsa\-65:
and its base64, for a repository's configuration.
.TP
.BI name " \fR[\fPname \fR[\fPclosure\fR]]\fP"
Name a closure in the closure directory, so that what a system runs can be
chosen by name.
Given a
.I name
and a
.IR closure ,
its identity as
.B closure
prints it, the name is set to that closure, replacing what it named.
Given only a
.IR name ,
print the identity of the closure it names; naming none is an error.
Given neither, print each name and the identity of its closure, one per
line, sorted by name.
With
.BR \-\-remove ,
remove the name, leaving its closure.
.IP
A name is a symbolic link beside the closures to the closure's tree,
replaced by renaming a new link over it, so it always names one closure or
another.
It is 1 to 64 ASCII letters, digits and
.BR \- ,
.B .\&
and
.BR _ ,
not starting with a dot, not ending in
.BR .manifest ,
and not a closure's hash.
An installed system names the closure that is its
.I /usr
.B SYSTEM
and the one its initramfs is built from
.BR INITRAMFS .
.TP
.BI compose\-env " \fR[\fPprofile\fR...]\fP"
Lay out the root of the environment that the
.I profile
files and the options describe in the new directory named by
.BR \-\-out ,
for a tool such as an image builder to take, and print the identity of the
packages' closure on standard output.
At least one of
.I profile
and
.B \-p
must be given, and the environment must hold a package.
The profiles' requests, and the exact IDs after
.BR \-p ,
are resolved against the artifact directory, with what they need.
The root's
.I usr
is the closure of the packages chosen, made for those requests, built in
place as
.B closure
builds it: every file is a hard link to its blob, in the root's own
artifact directory if it has one and otherwise in the artifact directory
the packages are read from, or a copy of that blob with
.BR \-\-copy\-files .
Nothing is written to that artifact directory.
.IP
What lies outside
.I usr
belongs to the environment, not to any package, except a link a package
claims there, such as
.IR /init ,
which the root holds because the package is in it.
Everything else outside
.I usr
comes from the profile's
.B symlinks
and
.BR dirs ,
.BR \-\-symlink ,
.BR \-\-dir ,
.BR \-\-include\-artifacts ,
and
.BR \-\-copy\-artifacts .
Their paths are relative to the root, must not contain an empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
component, and must not be
.I usr
or lie under it.
.IP
The root is built in a temporary directory beside it and renamed into
place when complete.
.B ppk compose\-env
fails, writing nothing, if the directory already exists, if the packages
clash as they would for
.BR closure ,
if a link claimed outside
.I usr
is also a link or directory of the environment, or if a path lies under a
symbolic link given or a link claimed.
It fails, leaving nothing behind, if a hard link cannot be made because the
directory is not on the same mount as the artifact directory, or if a blob
it copies does not match its hash.
To compose a root on another mount, give
.BR \-\-copy\-artifacts ,
or
.B \-\-copy\-files
if the root has no artifact directory of its own.
.TP
.BI run " \fR[\fPprofile\fR...] [\fP" \-\- " command \fR[\fPargument\fR...]]\fP"
Run
.IR command ,
or
.I /usr/bin/sh
when no command is given,
in an environment: the caller's own view of the filesystem, in a mount
namespace of its own, with the closure of the packages of the
.I profile
files and
.B \-p
mounted at
.IR /usr .
At least one of them must be given.
Requests after
.B \-p
accept the same names, constraints and exact IDs as
.BR fetch .
They are resolved against the artifact directory before composition,
together with the profiles' requests; an exact ID is a pin, and a request it
doesn't satisfy is an error rather than an upgrade.
The closure's inventory records the requests.
With
.BR \-\-no\-deps ,
only the exact IDs after
.B \-p
and the profiles' requests, which must each ask for one exact version, are
composed, without resolving dependencies or requirements.
Missing packages must be fetched first.
Everything outside
.I /usr
is as the caller sees it, so a profile's
.B symlinks
and
.B dirs
do not apply.
The closure is built as
.B closure
builds it, or reused if it exists.
.IP
.B ppk run
then replaces itself with
.IR command ,
whose exit status becomes
.BR ppk 's.
.I command
is looked up through
.B PATH
inside the environment, unless it contains a slash.
The default shell is the closure's own, not the caller's, so it is only
there if one of the packages provides
.I bin/sh
or claims
.IR /usr/bin/sh .
The mount stays inside the environment: nothing outside it sees the
closure at
.IR /usr ,
and the command's children share the environment.
.IP
.I /usr
must already exist; an environment replaces it rather than creating it.
.TP
.B resolve
Choose an exact version of each package the requests of
.B \-p
and
.B \-c
need, from the packages for the architecture in the
.B \-\-from
directories, and write their profile: an
.B arch
and a
.B packages
table holding one version of each name, with nothing else, so that a
fragment can add a layout.
A request is a name, then optionally a constraint, as one argument:
.B 'org.gnu.gcc >= 16'
or
.BR sh .
.IP
A dependency is on a package by its name.
A requirement is on a capability, which a package provides by its own name,
at its own version, or by an entry of its
.B provides
table, at that entry's version; an entry with no version satisfies any
constraint.
.B resolve
follows the dependencies and requirements of every package it chooses.
.IP
The choice is greedy, and nothing chosen is reconsidered.
Packages are settled before capabilities, at every step: the requests,
then the dependencies of what they chose, and only when none is left, the
first capability waiting.
A package is chosen at the newest version that satisfies what first needs
it.
A capability that a package already chosen provides needs nothing more;
otherwise the one package that provides it is chosen, at its newest
version that does.
.IP
.B resolve
fails, writing nothing, if a name has no version that satisfies what needs
it, if no package provides a capability, or if packages of several names
provide one, in which case a request must choose one of them by name.
It fails if something needs a version of a package that the one already
chosen doesn't satisfy, naming the requirement, the choice, and what it was
chosen for, and saying whether another version would have satisfied both.
It fails if the version it would choose is spelt two ways, as
.B 1.0~a\-1
and
.B 1.0~alpha\-1
are, since no constraint can tell them apart, or if one package is
different in two directories.
.TP
.BI help " \fR[\fPcommand\fR]\fP"
Print help for
.B ppk
or for one of its commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-all
For
.BR list ,
list the store's packages, marking those in the current closure.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-package " \fIpackage\fR..."
For
.B compose\-env
and for
.BR "run \-\-no\-deps" ,
add every
.I package
that follows, up to the next option, to the environment.
For
.BR run ,
request each package that follows, with its constraint or as an exact ID.
For
.BR resolve ,
request each package that follows, with its constraint.
Can be given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-capability " \fIrequest\fR..."
For
.BR resolve ,
request a package providing each capability that follows, with its
constraint.
Can be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch " arch"
For
.BR fetch ,
.BR run ,
and
.BR resolve ,
choose packages for
.IR arch ,
rather than for the architecture
.B ppk
was built for.
An exact ID with an explicit architecture must agree with this option.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-no\-deps .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-deps
For
.B fetch
and
.BR run ,
use exactly the package IDs given, without resolving dependencies or
requirements. IDs have the form
.IB name @ version\c
.RB [ ?arch= \c
.IR arch ].
Bare names and constraints are not accepted in this mode.
Profiles passed to run remain exact.
.TP
.BI \-\-artifact\-dir " dir"
Store packages in
.I dir
instead of
.IR /system/artifacts .
.B ppk
creates the directory if it does not exist.
For
.BR list ,
read this store with
.BR \-\-all ;
no directories are created.
.TP
.BI \-\-closure\-dir " dir"
For
.B closure
and
.BR run ,
keep closures in
.I dir
instead of the
.I closures
directory of the artifact directory.
.I dir
must be on the same mount as the artifact directory, unless
.B \-\-copy\-files
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-descriptor " path descriptor"
For
.BR pack ,
give the file or directory
.I path
of the package, as the manifest lists it, the security descriptor
.IR descriptor ,
in hexadecimal, as
.B SECURITY METADATA
describes.
.I path
and
.I descriptor
are separate arguments.
Can be given more than once, for different paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-depends " name constraint"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package depends on the package
.I name
at versions that satisfy
.IR constraint ,
or at any version if
.I constraint
is empty.
.I name
and
.I constraint
are separate arguments.
Can be given more than once, for different packages.
.TP
.BI \-\-requires " name constraint"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package requires the capability
.I name
at versions that satisfy
.IR constraint ,
or at any version if it is empty.
Can be given more than once, for different capabilities.
.TP
.BI \-\-provides " name version"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package provides the capability
.I name
at
.IR version ,
or at no particular version if it is empty.
Can be given more than once, for different capabilities.
.TP
.BI \-\-claims " path target"
For
.BR pack ,
record that the package claims the absolute
.I path
as a symbolic link to
.IR target ,
which is written into the link exactly as given.
.I path
has no empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
component and does not end in
.BR / .
Can be given more than once, for different paths.
.TP
.BI \-\-description " text"
For
.BR pack ,
record a one-line description, in printable ASCII.
.TP
.BI \-\-homepage " url"
For
.BR pack ,
record the upstream project's
.B http
or
.B https
URL.
.TP
.BI \-\-license " expression"
For
.BR pack ,
record the package's license, conventionally an SPDX expression.
.TP
.BI \-\-license\-class " class"
For
.BR pack ,
record which kind of terms the license is:
.BR unknown ,
.BR free ,
.BR firmware ,
or
.BR proprietary .
.B unknown
is the default, and is recorded as nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-source\-license " expression"
For
.BR pack ,
record the license of the source the package was built from.
.TP
.B \-\-copy\-files
For
.BR closure ,
.BR compose\-env ,
and
.BR run ,
copy each file of the tree from its blob rather than hard link it, so that
the tree can be on any mount.
A hard link cannot cross a mount, even between two mounts of one filesystem,
as a sandbox's bind mounts can be.
Each copy is checked against its hash as it is made, and has the mode and
security descriptor and security metadata of its blob, but as a file of its
own: it takes its own space, and a later change to the store's descriptors
does not reach it.
A closure that already exists is reused as it is.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " dir"
For
.BR fetch ,
copy from the artifact directory
.IR dir .
For
.BR resolve ,
choose from the packages in every artifact directory
.I dir
that follows, up to the next option.
Required.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " path"
For
.BR compose\-env ,
create the root at
.IR path ,
which must not exist.
Required.
For
.BR resolve ,
write the profile to the file
.IR path ,
replacing it, rather than to standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
For
.BR fetch ,
replace the package if the artifact directory already holds another
under its name: its ref then names the fetched identity, and the manifest
and blobs it named stay.
This breaks the rule that a stored version never changes.
It is for an artifact directory nothing relies on yet, such as a
bootstrap's, whose packages are rebuilt at the same versions before it is
published.
Requires
.BR \-\-no\-deps ,
so replacement affects only the named package.
.TP
.BI \-\-symlink " link"
For
.BR compose\-env ,
create a symbolic link, with
.I link
given as
.IB path : target\c
\&.
.I path
is where the link goes, and may not contain a colon.
.I target
is written into the link exactly as given, unless it starts with a second
colon, which stands for
.IR /usr :
.B init::sbin/prelude
links
.I init
to
.IR usr/sbin/prelude .
Such a target is relative, climbing back to the root from a link in a
subdirectory, so
.B a/b::bin/sh
links
.I a/b
to
.IR ../usr/bin/sh .
Missing parent directories are created.
Can be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
For
.BR compose\-env ,
create the empty directory
.IR path .
Can be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
For
.BR compose\-env ,
also give the root its own artifact directory,
.IR system/artifacts ,
holding the packages' refs, manifests, and blobs as hard links, so that
.B ppk
inside the environment finds them by default.
Only the packages composed are included, and not their closure.
The root must be on the same mount as the artifact directory.
.TP
.B \-\-copy\-artifacts
For
.BR compose\-env ,
give the root its own artifact directory as
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
does, but copy the packages into it, checking each as
.B fetch
does, so that the root can be on any mount.
The root's own files and its artifact directory then share nothing with
the artifact directory the packages are read from.
Copying is a way of including the packages, so this wins over
.B \-\-include\-artifacts
and a profile's
.BR include_artifacts .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program name and version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
For
.BR list ,
read
.I dir/usr/inventory
instead of
.IR /usr/inventory .
The default is
.IR / .
This does not change the store selected by
.BR \-\-artifact\-dir .
.SH SECURITY
.B ppk
makes no access checks of its own.
It needs write access to the artifact directory, and read access to the
directory being packed or the artifact directory being fetched from.
.PP
Every file
.B ppk
writes in the artifact directory gets a security descriptor whose
protected DACL grants Everyone read and execute, and SYSTEM and
Administrators full control.
Every directory it creates there, and the root of every closure tree, gets
the same grants, inherited by what is created inside it.
A file in a closure is a hard link, so it has its blob's descriptor.
The generated inventory has mode 0444 and inherits the composed tree's
descriptor, like directories created without manifest security metadata.
Only the DACL is set: the owner stays whoever ran
.BR ppk .
Off Peios, where the system call does not exist, no descriptor is set.
A file or directory given a descriptor of its own has that instead, as
.B SECURITY METADATA
describes.
.PP
.B run
needs no privilege on Peios: any process can create a mount namespace and
bind-mount into its own.
Reusing a closure only reads the store, so anyone can run a closure that
already exists; building one needs write access to the closure directory.
The closure is not mounted read-only, which needs a privilege; its files are
protected by their descriptors instead.
.PP
A package is readable by everyone on a machine it is stored on, so a
package must never contain a secret.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including printing help or the version.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as an unreadable file, an unwritable artifact directory, a
version that is already present with different contents, a package that
is missing from the source or fails a check, requests that
.B resolve
can't satisfy, or, for pack, an ELF object that cannot be read or a written
entry that
.B DERIVATION
refuses.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option, a missing argument, a malformed
.IR package ,
or, for
.BR pack ,
metadata that breaks a rule.
.TP
.B 126
For
.BR run ,
the command was found but could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
For
.BR run ,
the command was not found.
.PP
Once
.B run
has started its command, the exit status is the command's.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/inventory
The current closure's identity and packages, and what wants each.
The first line is
.B closure
and the closure's identity.
Each other line is a package's full ID, its identity, and a bracket for each
thing that wants it:
.B *
for a request of the closure itself, or the name of a package in the
closure; then, if what was asked for is a capability rather than the package
by name, the capability; then, for a request from an index or channel,
.B #
and its name; then the constraint, one token per expression, such
as
.BR >=8.22 ,
and none for any version:
.IP
.EX
closure sha256:9c41...
org.openssl.libssl@3.5.8\-1?arch=x86_64 sha256:... [se.curl.curl libssl.so.3]
se.curl.curl@8.22.0\-1?arch=x86_64 sha256:... [* >=8.22]
.EE
.IP
A package is wanted by every request, and by every dependency or requirement
of a package in the closure, that it satisfies.
Lines are sorted by package ID, and brackets by requester,
.B *
first, then by capability, none first.
The closure's identity is the SHA-256 hash of every line after the first.
.PP
Paths are relative to the artifact directory,
.I /system/artifacts
by default.
.TP
.IR blobs/sha256/ hash
The content of one file that is not executable, uncompressed, named by the
SHA-256 hash of that content.
Mode 0444.
.TP
.IR blobs/sha256/ hash .exec
The same for an executable file.
Mode 0555.
Content shipped both as executable and not is stored in both forms.
.TP
.IR blobs/sha256/ hash . entry
The same content for an entry with security metadata, which it carries.
.I entry
is the SHA-256 hash of the entry as the manifest writes it, after its path.
Mode 0444, or 0555 if the entry is executable.
.TP
.IR manifests/sha256/ hash
A manifest, named by the SHA-256 hash of its bytes.
.TP
.IR refs/ name / version / arch
The identity of the package with that name, version, and architecture,
followed by a newline.
.TP
.IR closures/ hash
The tree of the closure with that identity, unless
.B \-\-closure\-dir
names another directory.
.TP
.IR closures/ name
A name for a closure: a symbolic link to its tree beside it, set by
.BR name .
.TP
.IR /conf/ppk/repositories/ name .toml
A repository this machine fetches from:
.IP
.EX
path = "/mnt/peios\-repo"
key = "ml\-dsa\-65:..."
track = "stable"
.EE
.IP
.B path
is required;
.B key
and
.B track
are optional.
.TP
.IR /var/state/ppk/sequences/ name
The highest sequence this machine has seen of the repository
.IR name .
.TP
.I repository
A published repository's pointer file, the one file of it that changes:
.IP
.EX
sequence 1042
signed 2026\-11\-13T11:30:00Z
catalog sha256:...
index 2026.10.0 sha256:...
index leading sha256:...
channel stable 2026.10
channel 2026.09 2026.09.3 ended
.EE
.IP
.B sequence
goes up by one each time the repository is published, and
.B signed
is when, in UTC.
.B catalog
and each
.B index
name the top node of a tree;
each
.B channel
names the index or channel it follows, and
.B ended
marks one that won't move again.
Lines are in this order, indexes and channels each sorted by name.
.TP
.I repository.sig
The ML-DSA-65 signature of the pointer file's bytes, in the context
.BR "ppk repository" .
.TP
.IR trees/sha256/ hash
A node of the catalog's or an index's tree, named by the SHA-256 hash of its
content.
A package's node has a line for each version:
the version, the architecture and the manifest's identity, then
.BR yanked ,
or
.B deleted
and a reason, for a version taken back.
A bucket's node has a line for each package whose name falls in it: its
name and its node's hash.
The top node has a line for each bucket: the bucket and its node's hash.
A name falls in the bucket of the first two hex digits of the SHA-256 hash
of the name.
Every node's lines are sorted and unique, each ending in a newline.
A catalog lists every version published; an index, one of each package for
each architecture.
.TP
.IR closures/ hash .manifest
The manifest of that closure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ppk pack 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' hello \-\-artifact\-dir artifacts
Pack the directory
.I hello
into
.I artifacts
and print the package's identity.
The identifier is quoted because some shells treat
.B ?
as a wildcard.
.TP
.B ppk pack org.git.kernel.dash@0.5.13.5\-1 dash \-\-depends org.gnu.glibc '>= 2.44' \-\-provides sh '' \-\-claims /usr/bin/sh dash
Pack the directory
.I dash
as a package that depends on glibc 2.44 or later, provides the capability
.BR sh ,
and claims
.I /usr/bin/sh
as a link to
.IR dash .
.TP
.B ppk fetch org.figlet.figlet \-\-from shared \-\-artifact\-dir artifacts
Copy the package from the artifact directory
.I shared
into
.I artifacts
with everything it needs, printing each fetched identity, which is the one
.B ppk pack
printed.
.TP
.B ppk closure 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' 'dev.peios.greetings@2.0\-1?arch=noarch'
Compose the two packages into a closure in
.I /system/artifacts/closures
and print its identity.
.TP
.B ppk run \-p 'dev.peios.hello@1.0\-1?arch=noarch' \-\- /usr/bin/hello
Run
.I /usr/bin/hello
from the package, in an environment whose
.I /usr
is the package's closure.
.TP
.B ppk compose\-env initramfs.toml \-\-out initramfs
Lay out the root of an initramfs in
.IR initramfs ,
from the profile shown under
.BR PROFILES :
the two packages as its
.IR usr ,
.I init
linked to
.IR usr/sbin/prelude ,
and empty
.IR proc ,
.IR sys ,
and
.I tmp
directories.
.TP
.B ppk compose\-env initramfs.toml \-p dev.peios.ppk@0.1.0\-1 \-\-out initramfs
The same, with
.B ppk
added to the profile's packages.
.TP
.B ppk resolve \-p org.gnu.make 'org.gnu.gcc >= 16' \-c sh \-\-from stage1 \-\-out root.toml
Choose make, a gcc of version 16 or later, a package providing
.BR sh ,
and everything they need, from the artifact directory
.IR stage1 ,
and write their profile to
.IR root.toml .
.TP
.B ppk run \-p net.busybox.busybox@1.37.0\-1
Start the shell that the busybox package provides, in an environment
whose
.I /usr
is that package's closure.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://learn.peios.org/peios/developing-for-peios/packaging/creating-a-package
